  <text>
    <body>
      <p>The Sands &amp; McDougall directories list five schools in the project area between 1857 and 1940, from the National School in <ref target="civic?id=Madeline%20Street">Madeline Street</ref> in the 1850s to State School No. 2365 in <ref target="civic?id=Queensberry%20Street">Queensberry Street</ref> and the Carlton Free Kindergarten in <ref target="civic?id=Bouverie%20Street">Bouverie Street</ref>. Many more small schools and teachers were listed under the name of the person who ran them, usually from their own home.</p>
      <p>The lists below are drawn only from the directories. Each school's name links to its own entry, and most of them haven't been written yet. If you know a school's story, please start its entry.</p>
      <div>
        <head>Schools in the directories</head>
        <p>Years are the first and last directories that list the school; the directories are yearly to 1880 and then every five years. Where different schools are listed at the same number, they are kept apart and noted.</p>
        <table>
          <row><cell><hi rend="bold">School</hi></cell><cell><hi rend="bold">Listed in</hi></cell><cell><hi rend="bold">Numbers</hi></cell><cell><hi rend="bold">Years</hi></cell><cell><hi rend="bold">Notes</hi></cell></row>
          <row><cell><ref target="civic?id=National%20School%20%28Madeline%20Street%29">National School</ref></cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Madeline%20Street">Madeline Street</ref></cell><cell>63, 77, 65</cell><cell>1857–1868</cell><cell>West side. Listed as "National Schools" in 1860. No. 65 is later listed as John Donaghy's school (1869–1872) and W. Bailey's school (1873).</cell></row>
          <row><cell><ref target="civic?id=State%20School%20No.%202365">State School No. 2365</ref></cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Queensberry%20Street">Queensberry Street</ref></cell><cell></cell><cell>1885–1935</cell><cell>North side. Head teachers or caretakers are listed with it. Listed as the "Training School" in 1910 and "State school" in 1915.</cell></row>
          <row><cell><ref target="civic?id=University%20School">University School</ref></cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Bouverie%20Street">Bouverie Street</ref>, <ref target="civic?id=Grattan%20Street">Grattan Street</ref>, <ref target="civic?id=Alfred%20Cottages">Alfred Cottages</ref></cell><cell></cell><cell>1873–1874</cell><cell>Thomas M. (or J. M.) Wilson. Listed under all three, probably at the corner of Bouverie and Grattan streets.</cell></row>
          <row><cell><ref target="civic?id=Carlton%20Free%20Kindergarten">Carlton Free Kindergarten</ref></cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Bouverie%20Street">Bouverie Street</ref>, <ref target="civic?id=Banbridge%20Place">Banbridge Place</ref></cell><cell>158, 233; 233a Banbridge Place</cell><cell>1915–1940</cell><cell>Miss M. Wilson, superintendent (1915). At first at 158 Bouverie Street, the number of the <ref target="civic?id=Baptist%20mission%20hall%20%28Bouverie%20Street%29">Baptist mission hall</ref> (see <ref target="civic?id=Churches">Churches</ref>); at 233 Bouverie Street and 233a Banbridge Place by 1930. See also <ref target="civic?id=Kindergartens">Kindergartens</ref>.</cell></row>
          <row><cell><ref target="civic?id=A.B.%20Engineering%20Schools">A.B. Engineering Schools</ref></cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Bouverie%20Street">Bouverie Street</ref></cell><cell>131–35</cell><cell>1940</cell><cell>West side. "Engng Schools (of Aust), engnrg tchrs".</cell></row>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Private schools and teachers</head>
        <p>These schools and teachers are listed under a person's name. They are given here as the directories print them.</p>
        <table>
          <row><cell><hi rend="bold">Listed as</hi></cell><cell><hi rend="bold">Listed in</hi></cell><cell><hi rend="bold">Numbers</hi></cell><cell><hi rend="bold">Years</hi></cell></row>
          <row><cell>Goodridge, Thomas T., school</cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Madeline%20Street">Madeline Street</ref></cell><cell>67, 83</cell><cell>1857–1860</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Combes, Mrs., school</cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Pelham%20Street">Pelham Street</ref></cell><cell></cell><cell>1860–1861</cell></row>
          <row><cell><ref target="civic?id=George%20Zeplin">Zeplin, George, professor of music</ref></cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Queensberry%20Street">Queensberry Street</ref></cell><cell>40, 34</cell><cell>1860–1862</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Mira, Miss, teacher of music</cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Leicester%20Street">Leicester Street</ref></cell><cell>55</cell><cell>1864–1865</cell></row>
          <row><cell>James, Mrs., school</cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Barry%20Street">Barry Street</ref></cell><cell></cell><cell>1866</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Palmer, Mrs., ladies' school</cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Berkley%20Street">Berkley Street</ref></cell><cell>60</cell><cell>1869</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Morey, Miss, school</cell><cell><ref target="civic?id=Berkley%20Terrace">Berkley Terrace</ref></cell><cell>3</cell><cell>1869</cell></row>
